<!-- Website teaching layer; original arrays-and-strings.md remains unchanged. -->
<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A product search checks every pair of products to find two prices that add up to a gift-card value. With 10,000 products that is about 50 million pairs. A sorted array and two pointers, or one hash set, does it in a single pass.</p>

Arrays give constant-time access by index because elements sit next to each other in memory. Most array interview patterns trade that layout for fewer passes: move two indices instead of nesting two loops, keep a running window instead of recomputing it, or precompute prefix sums once.

<!-- @before Core Patterns :: guide -->
### Pick the pattern from the question's shape

- **Pairs in sorted data** (two values meeting a target): two pointers moving inward, O(n) after sorting.
- **Best contiguous range** (longest substring, maximum window): sliding window that grows on the right and shrinks on the left, O(n).
- **Many range-sum queries**: prefix sums, O(n) once, then O(1) per query.
- **Counting or matching characters**: a fixed-size count array or a hash map instead of nested scans.

<!-- @after Core Patterns :: check -->
**Choose the pattern.** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Longest substring without repeating characters.</summary>

Sliding window with a map of each character's last index. When a repeat appears inside the window, move the left edge past its previous position. Each index enters and leaves the window at most once: O(n).
</details>

<details><summary>Product of the array except self, without division.</summary>

Two passes of running products: left-to-right products before each index, then multiply by right-to-left products after it. O(n) time and O(1) extra space besides the output.
</details>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->
**Review question to ask:** "What is the largest input this runs on in production?" An O(n²) scan over a 20-item list is fine; the same code over a chat history or contact list is a latent ANR.
